e  so specifically today i'm going to be  talking to you about the diagnostics  aspect  and  the proteins that mammoth uses are ideal  for diagnostics due to a few key  features  one of them is the programmable nature  of the platform where  by modifying the guide rna associated  with the cast protein you can target any  nucleic acid of interest  furthermore these proteins exhibit rapid  enzymatic recognition and so they very  quickly are able to find their target  and convert that into a signal which can  lead to rapid diagnostic assays  additionally due to the requirement for  the guide rna to hybridize to the target  there is a high molecular accuracy and  so all of this can lead to very simple  and specific assays that can be used for  diagnostics  and so for diagnostics in particular the  way that  many of these proteins work is that  once the protein is programmed by the  guide rna to target a specific sequence  once the  guide rna hybridizes to that sequence  and recognizes it then the complex is  activated and that activation then  unleashes  non-specific nucleus activity where the  enzyme begins to non-specifically cleave  other nucleic acids that happen to be  nearby  this can be leveraged in order  to  perform diagnostic applications because  you can use a reporter molecule that you  introduce into the solution  and that has a nucleic acid sequence  that you desire to be cleaved in order  to produce a signal  so for example you can have a  fluorophore molecule that's separated  from a clenching molecule by a nucleic  acid sequence and so when the sequence  is cleaved  in the presence of target that  fluorophore is released from its  quencher enabling the solution to become  fluorescent  so here is just a video so the cast  protein is complexed with its guide rna  and that complex is then able to  travel  through solution and the guide rna  programs the complex to to bind to its  target of interest  and after binding and recognition the  complex is then activated and it begins  to non-specifically cleave any other  nucleic acids that happen to be nearby  in this case this is again demonstrated  using a fluorophore quencher model where  you have a fluorophore separated by a  nucleic acid from a quencher and upon  cleavage that fluorophore is released  and becomes fluorescent allowing you to  measure the fluorescence of the solution  to determine  whether the target was present  and so this crispr detector platform can  be programmed to target any dna or rna  sequence of interest  that can be all the way from an  infectious pathogen to a cancer mutation  so  simply by changing the guide rna that is  associated with the protein  it gives a great flexibility as far as  diagnostic targets these targets could  be anything from viruses or bacteria  or even genotyping if you are interested  in identifying a gene mutation  or looking at host response  and so these  detector crispr caps platform has a few  key advantages on that are useful for  diagnostic assays looking for nucleic  acids  so one of these is that because the cast  protein is interrogating the  complementarity between the guide rna  and the target rna  there is increased specificity when you  compare to a traditional nucleic acid  amplification test  for example if you're looking at tacman  hybridization probes for comparison  furthermore these cast proteins have a  native signal amplification and the  protein performs multiple cleavage  events turning over  multiple reporter molecules per  identification of a single target and so  this can result in robust signal  generation from small amounts of  amplicon  additionally because of the specificity  of the cast protein it can sort through  messy isothermal amplification products  isothermal amplification tends to  be a bit messy and oftentimes it will  produce some non-specific products in  addition to the specific product you're  looking for  but when coupled with a cast enzyme the  cast enzyme can lead to a simplified and  faster workflow and fewer false  positives due to its specificity  and finally because the signal  generation is just dependent on nucleic  acid cleavage  the reporter  can be modified to  enable a variety of different readout  modalities and so you're not limited to  just fluorescence but you can have very  flexible formats for deploying your  diagnostic assay  and so as i mentioned  these detector  enzymes can lead to  increased signal to noise when you  compare to just doing an isothermal  amplification alone  and so on the left are some example  kinetic curves looking at  just isothermal amplification and you  can see that it's really hard to tell a  difference between the different wells  and all of the curves look fairly  similar but then when you couple this  with  a  crisper detection as shown on the right  the specificity of the crisper cast  complex leads  to  good differentiation in between your  positive and negative wells  and in this case the positive wells are  shown in orange and the negative in red  and you can see there's a very distinct  signal difference in between the two  furthermore the specificity of this  detector system  can  enable  even detection even down to single  nucleotide differences if you're looking  for example  single nucleotide polymorphism  and so the  image on the top illustrates this by  showing  a target that has been mutated in  various locations and the  the target that was designed but the  guide rna was designed for is shown by  the boxes  in pink  whereas  there was a comparison to different  point mutations mutating to the possible  different nucleotides at all the  different locations and we see that the  crispr cast complex has  really good specificity identifying its  target  and it's resistant to  a single nucleotide change all the way  from  the region right next to the pam down to  the very end of the spacer region  and so we can leverage this at mammoth  in order to  be able to genotype and identify  differences between a full wild-type  gene a heterozygous or a full mutant  and we can  compare the signal and very easily  stratify  into different buckets and allow us to  determine for the samples shown along  the bottom whether they were a wild type  heterozygous or a mutant  additionally because the detector  platform is compatible with different  readout modalities it can  enable application to  different types of diagnostic  technologies  and so i talked a bit about fluorescence  previously as a readout but the assay  can also be coupled to a lateral flow  readout  where you have in  response to the cleavage event of the  crispr cast complex  you generate a different signal on a  lateral flow strip and so you can  observe this change by eye similar to  the way you would for example in a home  pregnancy test  additionally the crispr pass  complex can be coupled with a secondary  enzyme that will generate a colorimetric  change and so in this case you can have  a change in color in response to the  presence of target and that change in  color can be identified by eye  and a final option is to do  electrochemical based detection using  instead a reporter that is  electrochemically active and exhibits a  different electrochemical signal after  cleavage  all of these different advantages of  crispr enables it to have broad  application across the full continuum of  testing all the way from laboratory test  to at-home tests  and the programmable nature of the  detector platform means they can use it  to detect any different indications  while its high specificity and  sensitivity  provides great usefulness in very  different  diagnostic settings  and so this can be useful both on a  laboratory basis where  the goal is to increase throughput  and  using for example automated tests in  order to run as many tests as possible  all the way from that to  on at home tests where  you're interested in having a single use  disposable device where someone could  test  at their own home  and in order to do this you need  something that is low cost  and very rapid so  the person doesn't have to wait a long  time when running the test as well as be  able to be distributed into homes  and so  the  current coven 19 pandemic has really  validated the need for rapid and  accurate diagnostic tests that can be  quickly deployed and mammoth worked very  rapidly in response to  the beginning of the pandemic  mammoth first developed an assay very  rapidly in response to the publication  of the full viral sequence in january of  2020  and we worked with clinical  collaborators at ucsf in order to  develop first a peer-reviewed manuscript  that was published in nature  biotechnology in april of 2020.  we further were able to obtain  partnerships  with  gfk and with darpa to develop  decentralized testing options as well as  some funding from the nih in  partnerships with milicar sigma hamilton  and agilent  in order to develop a lab-based test  called detector boost  and  all of the efforts surrounding the assay  has enabled us to go from paper to  accelerated product development in under  six months  i'm going to talk to you a little bit  about both the lab-based tests as well  as decentralized testing  but first i would like to give an  overview of the essay itself which was  published in a manuscript in  biotechnology  in  april of 2020  and this was the world's first  peer-reviewed manuscript demonstrating  the use of crispr diagnostics for covet  19.  and so in response to  the  pandemic mammoth scientists worked very  quickly in order to develop an assay  that could leverage crispr and use it to  detect stars cov2  and so we developed two different  essays for two different  pieces of the viral genome one is the  e gene region  where the the guide rna that we  developed overlaps with the w-h-o-e  amplicon  and also an assay for the n-gene region  where the guide rna overlaps with the  cdc and two amplicon  and so the first step in developing the  essay was to make sure  that the guide rnas were specific for  the target of interest and didn't have  cross reactivity and so the graph in the  bottom left  shows a comparison  of  the guides for the engine and aegean  target  as well as rna-sp which we use as a  sample input control  and we see that the n-gene and the  e-gene guides are both very specific for  that particular target and don't show  cross-reactivity with some other common  coronaviruses  whereas the rnap or the sample input  control is present across the different  coronavirus samples  furthermore mammoth leveraged a lateral  flow based readout in this test in order  to eliminate the need for bulky  fluorescence equipment  and this lateral flow test  will  determine based on a visual readout  the result of the assay  and so as the cast complex is activated  it cleaves the reporter that when  the  product of the reaction is put on a  lateral flow strip if the reporter has  been cleaved then it generates a  different signal compared to if the  target was not present and the reporter  was not cleaved  and so the crisper portion of the assay  works incredibly rapidly and so the  graph near the bottom right shows the  signal  after different reaction times of the  crispr part of the reaction and we see  that  almost instantaneously you can see a  difference in between the positive and  negative samples  the lateral flow readout is shown in the  bottom right and we see that we can  start to distinguish about two and a  half minutes but after five minutes you  really see  a strong difference in between your  positive and negative  mammoth further validated the test by  collaborating with ucsf and testing on  real clinical samples  and so this slide shows a comparison  from tests with 83 different clinical  samples  and so if the  both the engine and e-gene assays were  positive for stars cov2 then the sample  was called positive  if either one of the n or ng and or a  gene were  positive then the sample was presumptive  positive whereas if both the ng and a  gene were negative and the rna p control  as a sample input control was positive  then  the sample was called stars kobe 2  negative  and using that scheme we were able to  obtain  a positive percent agreement  of 95 and a negative percent agreement  of 100  illustrating the  applicability of crispr to  real clinical samples in response to the  emerging pandemic  and so mammoths further leveraged the  assay in order to  develop a lab-based  test that could  impact  high-throughput testing that's done in  diagnostic laboratories so that is the  detector boost starts 2 reagent kit  this is in partnership with agilent and  their bravo instrument as well as  hamilton and their star instrument  and  it has the potential of increasing  testing capacity up to thousands of  tests per day due to  the simple and rapid crispr test  and as a result of this can get results  faster up to two times faster than pcr  workflows  furthermore because the assay is very  simple there is less hands-on time it's  less than an hour which  reduces the fte load and can allow  employees to  focus on other tests while increasing  diagnostic throughput  and finally it has a performance similar  to rt-pcr with an lod of 150 copies per  milliliter  so in comparison to rtpcr the detector  boost assay takes approximately 90  minutes per 96 well plate in order to  run whereas rtpcr takes about 140  minutes per 96 well plate and also  involves some manual steps  for the detector boost assay  we have a duplexed assay with thyroscope  v2 engine as well as rnasp is an  internal control  and it displays an analytical log at 150  copies per milliliter  furthermore in a clinical study with 230  nasopharyngeal swabs  we were able to obtain 100  positive percent agreement  with in samples that had a comparator ct  that was less than our estimated lg  so in addition to applying the crispr  detector platform to laboratory-based  testing mammoth has also worked to  develop a decentralized testing platform  and so for the sam the decentralized  prototype it's able to  perform the full assay in less than 40  minutes including extraction  amplification and the detector crispr  reaction  and so to develop this prototype we  integrated the assay chemistry onto  a prototype device that only requires a  single user step  and allows for sample to answer in just  40 minutes  mammoth tested this on 100 different  prototypes and found that we were able  to obtain 96  overall agreement for detection of stars  cov2  we've manufactured more than 500 of  these prototypes  furthermore these prototypes also  convert the signal from the assay into a  positive negative indicator light in  order for the results to be unambiguous  to the user  and so at the end of the assay there is  a measurement cycle that is initiated  and then the data is collected  and an algorithm calls the result and  then based on that result  the appropriate light is  is lit up whether that's a positive or a  negative call  and as i mentioned we tested this on a  hundred different prototype devices and  found that we were able to obtain a  positive agreement of 100  and a negative agreement of 92 with  overall agreement of 96  and so all of this illustrates the  applicability of crispr and  the advantages of  having the highly specific and rapid  testing that can really impact the  future of diagnostics and can enable  molecular testing that is in the palm of  your hand  and with that i'd like to thank the  entire mammoth bio sciences team mammoth  has a lot of great people who work here  and it's a pleasure to come to work with  them every day  as well as i'd like to  give a shout out to all of our  collaborators including ucsf for the  nature biotech paper and the clinical  studies as well as the many other  collaborators on the various projects we  do at mammoth thank you so much for  listening to my talk

